# Never send local credentials or environment overrides to Docker.
#
# Docker's ignore syntax is NOT gitignore: patterns are matched against paths relative to the
# context root, and only a leading `**/` matches at any depth. A bare `.env` therefore protects
# the repository root and nothing below it, which is not enough here, because the build context
# is the repo root (docker/compose.yaml `context: ..`) and `COPY . /bench` takes all of it.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example

# Three tracked FIXTURES look like credentials and are not: ts-glob-hidden is a task about
# globbing dotfiles, so its planted .env.production is the thing under test, and the in-image
# `pytest tests/` needs them. Re-included by name, after the rule above, because a later pattern
# wins.
!oracles/**/.env.production
!tasks/**/.env.production

# The image only needs source, fixtures, and the test harness. Never copy local history,
# generated run data, caches, logs, or credential-bearing exports into image layers.
# `.claude/` holds session state, project-scoped MCP config and, on a developer machine, entire
# worktree checkouts; `**/.git` catches the nested repositories those worktrees carry.
.git
**/.git
.claude/
.mcp.json
**/*.local.json
.github
results/
staging/
corpus/conditions/
*.log
*.secrets.json
pricing.json
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.venv/
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/

# The generated haystack: gitignored, up to ~24 MB of transcripts per scale, and reproducible
# from `scripts/generate_haystack.py`. `.gitignore` excluded it and this file did not, and
# `docker/Dockerfile.harness` is `COPY . /bench`, so every local haystack root went into the
# image layers. Docker ignore is not gitignore: this pattern is relative to the context root.
corpus/haystack/
